Got to the root cause: CSV price updates break the assumption of menu versions being immutable.

Here’s an example:

Vendor menu version: {{4503599627813482}}

{code:sql}select *
from vendor_menu_version
where id = 4503599627813482;

/*
topology.item_data: 7901 items
last_update: 2024-05-17 07:17:19.0
*/{code}

This menu version is assigned to the following providers:

{code:sql}select *
from provider_menu_version
where menu_version_id = 4503599627813482;
/*
providers: 74099, 74103, 74102
last_update: 2024-01-17
*/{code}

So the version was changed after being assigned to providers (4 months after!).

Diving a little deeper into Kibana logs using [this filter|https://kibana-logs.elasticsearch.boltint.net/app/r/s/lfJuu]:

{noformat}query.additional_parameters : "4503599627813482"{noformat}

At {{17/05/2024 07:17:18}} we get a call to:

{noformat}/deliveryMenuManagement/menuItemAvailabilityUpdateCsvUpload/{noformat}

So, we already had the menu version in ETL tables (7901 rows), but then a CSV update happened which updated in place just a few items, hence the {{Found previous menu version in ETL with a different number of items than current menu version}} error in the ETL cronjob.

Given that this immutability break is expected & planned to be deprecated there’s nothing to be done in the ETL cronjob, except for removing the error message.

